// PENDING (K1): pins the SD card's number in the two device trees, so U-Boot's index and Linux's /dev/mmcblkN each follow
// from an alias and cannot disagree by probe order. k230.dtsi (SDK U-Boot overlay, arch/riscv/dts/k230.dtsi:34-36) has an
// aliases block with only uart0.
//
// U-Boot dts (the one recorded edit on top of the pristine vendor dts): the SD host is U-Boot's mmc 1, the number the boot
// mode SYSCTL_BOOT_SDIO1 selects (board/canaan/common/k230_spl.c:65, :167), and the SD node is mmc1@91581000 (k230.dtsi:163).
//   aliases { mmc1 = &mmc1; };
//
// Kernel dts (our AI-CAM dts): the SD host is Linux's mmc 0, as the mainline kernel dts pins it (Gaia's AI-CAM dts,
// examples/boards/k230d-ai-cam/assets/dts/k230d-ai-cam.dts:37-40, aliases mmc0 = &mmc_sd1), so the root is /dev/mmcblk0p2
// and the env disk is /dev/mmcblk0. The alias is the SD host's label in our kernel dts. Its NODE NAME must be sdhci1@91581000
// for Lemnos's boot_sources path (see docs/prs/K1-k230d.md, node names): Gaia's node is mmc@91581000 today, so it is a mismatch.
//   aliases { mmc0 = &sdhci1; };
//
// CONFIRM ON BOARD before the first Lemnos write: /dev/mmcblk0 is the SD disk, and the U-Boot index is 1. A wrong device makes
// Lemnos refuse ("no valid environment"); it never writes blindly.
